<p>Due to an illness in the family, I've spent rather less time in the studio than usual this summer. I also had to cut short one show and I missed another entirely.</p>
<p>Fortunately, everyone's now back to full health.</p>
<p>Here are some of the paintings that I managed to do between June and the end of August:</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><img style="display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;" src="/images/2019/Pasture-on-Picnic-Woods-Road.jpg" alt="Oil painting of horses grazing in a summer meadow against a backdrop of dark trees" width="650" height="340" /></p>
<p style="text-align: center;">Grazing on Axline Road. 12x24, oil on hemp. 2019</p>
<p>I based this painting on some reference photos from 2014 or 2015. When I lived in Virginia, I would have been running away from all that green, but from the blue-saturated environment of Seattle it feels rather welcoming.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><img style="display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;" src="/images/2019/Grazing-on-Axline-Road2.jpg" alt="Abstract impressionistic oil painting of horses grazing" width="650" height="328" /></p>
<p style="text-align: center;">Grazing on Axline Road. 6x12, oil on linen panel. 2019.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">I also painted a smaller, more abstract version of the same scene. Normally I would paint the small picture first, but this backwards way of working seems to suit me.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><img style="display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;" src="/images/2019/Breakers2.jpg" alt="Oil painting of waves at the beach" width="650" height="393" /></p>
<p style="text-align: center;">Breakers. 14x24, oil on linen. 2019.</p>
<p>Another seascape to add to the collection that I created in the spring.</p>
<p>The larger size of this painting makes it harder to maintain the same level of abstract mark making that you can get in smaller pieces. To compensate, I worked in layers to create a mosaic of paint colors in any given area.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><img style="display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;" src="/images/2019/Late-Winter-Fields2.jpg" alt="Oil painting of dark trees against a winter sky" width="650" height="527" /></p>
<p style="text-align: center;">Late Winter Fields. 16x20, oil on linen. 2019</p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><span style="text-align: start;">&mdash;I have made some changes to this painting since it was first posted&mdash;</span></p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Based on some smaller studies I did last year, this is part of the Magnolia skyline (looking from the Whole Foods parking lot!) with a bit of impressionistic foreground added.</p>
<p>Paintings like this are simple in composition but technically difficult to create&mdash;you have to use a great deal of restraint in order to let the initial layers show through: once you paint over that thin underpainting, it's gone.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><img style="display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;" src="/images/2019/Puget-Sands.jpg" alt="Oil painting of low tide on Puget Sound" width="650" height="362" /></p>
<p style="text-align: center;">Puget Sands. 9x16, oil on linen panel. 2019.</p>
<p>&nbsp;A simple theme, this one based on a smaller painting I did earlier in the year. Like most seascapes, there's actually not much going on in the scene&mdash;here I just used the shoreline as a backdrop to showcase the cloud formations.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><img style="display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;" src="/images/2019/Spring-Tide2.jpg" alt="Abstract impressionistic oil painting of a beach scene" width="650" height="487" /></p>
<p style="text-align: center;">Spring Tide. 9x12, oil on linen panel. 2019.</p>
<p>I did this final painting on a standard size panel rather than use the elongated ones I've come to rely on for seascapes. It puts far more emphasis on the foreground, and that's where I ended up spending most of my time.</p>
<p>I used several layers, developing each area as I went so that the final picture eventually looked right to my eye.</p>
